WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.400
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
as what to feed ourselves
and our families,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.760
we should rely not on
anecdotes, but on facts.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.600
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.640
Today we feature the strangely
complex art of smoothie making!

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.320
As it turns out,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.400
if you make your
smoothies the wrong way

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.600
you can partially destroy
valuable phytonutrients.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.800
Here’s our first story.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.560
There are
recommended daily intakes

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.760
of essential nutrients
like vitamins

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.840
to prevent deficiencies,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.560
but recently the first
dietary recommendation

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.600
for a bioactive food compound
was published.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.920
Bioactives are food compounds
that are not necessary

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.400
for basic human needs

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.640
but may have health benefits;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.200
for example, plant metabolites
called flavonoids.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.440
Flavonoids are
a type of polyphenol,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.160
of which the most commonly
consumed are flavan-3-ols,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.360
also known as just flavanols.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.920
A review of the literature
suggested that the intake

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.880
of 400 to 600 mg a day
of flavanols

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.520
would be beneficial
for cardiometabolic protection,

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.120
potentially helping to improve
blood pressure, cholesterol,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.640
and blood sugars.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.840
Note, it's specified
that these are food guidelines,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.960
not supplement guidelines,
though really,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.960
they're more like
beverage guidelines

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.840
as tea—green or black—
would be by far

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.880
the easiest way
to reach those levels,

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.200
though there are
also certainly some

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.320
in a variety
of berries and cocoa.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.600
However, there is an enzyme
called polyphenol oxidase

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.040
that can break down polyphenols
like flavanols.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.520
That's what causes certain
fruits and vegetables to brown

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.480
when you cut into them,
like bananas and potatoes.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.080
Why would a plant
do that to itself?

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.520
Apparently
as a defense mechanism,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.240
part of the plant's
own immune system.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.800
When exposed to oxygen,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.680
polyphenol oxidase
can oxidize polyphenols

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.840
into breakdown products
with antimicrobial activity.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.320
So, when a banana gets bruised
or you cut open an avocado,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.880
the polyphenol oxidase
can start oxidizing polyphenols

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.800
into defensive compounds
that can glom together

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.160
and form a brown compound
called melanin.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.000
The same class of compounds that
darkens the skin of a banana

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
darkens our skin, too.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.200
Okay, no problem, we can just
not eat brown bananas,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.960
as that's visual evidence

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.120
that many of the polyphenols
have been lost.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.480
But what if you mixed
that banana,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.400
which you know is filled with
the polyphenol oxidase enzyme,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.920
into a smoothie
with polyphenol-rich foods,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.880
like berries or cocoa?

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.520
Might you end up
with less nutrition?

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.120
Big Chocolate-funded researchers
sought to find out.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.120
They put about half a cup (40 g)
of cocoa's worth

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.160
of cocoa flavanols into
a smoothie containing banana,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.160
compared to the same amount

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.160
in a berry smoothie
with no bananas.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.040
Here's a measure of how many
of the chocolate flavanols

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.600
made it into the blood stream

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.640
within hours of drinking
the chocolatey berry smoothie,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.040
compared to
the same amount of cocoa

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.120
blended into a banana smoothie.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.600
It's almost as if you hardly put
any cocoa in there at all,

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.040
presumably because
the banana enzymes chopped up

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.040
all the chocolate goodies.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.120
Let's not presume, though.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.840
They blended up the chocolatey
banana smoothie again—

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.200
banana, almond milk,
and cocoa flavanols—

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.040
and then just measured
the amount of flavanols

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.360
as the smoothie sat for an hour.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:32.520
Check it out.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.960
The cocoa flavanols plummeted,
more than a 90 percent drop,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.760
a half-life of, like,
10 minutes,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.360
so every 10 minutes or so,
the levels drop in half.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.040
How do they know for sure it was
the polyphenol oxidase enzyme?

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.760
Because when they added
specific enzyme blockers,

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.880
they blocked the effect.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:51.880
Okay, no problem.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.600
If you're adding bananas
to your smoothies,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.440
can't you just chug it quickly
before the enzyme does its job?

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.160
Presumably,
the enzyme would be deactivated

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.160
when it hit the acid bath
of the stomach,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.720
but let's not presume.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:08.760
You don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.800
I love that the researchers did
all these extra steps.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:15.560
This time they made study
participants alternate sips

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.960
between a straight
banana-almond milk smoothie

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:19.400
and, like, chocolate milk,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.840
cocoa flavanols
plus almond milk.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.880
So, there was
no premixing in the blender

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.920
between the banana enzyme
and the flavanols,

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.960
just mixing in the stomach.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.800
Here's how many cocoa flavanols
make it into your bloodstream

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.040
when sipping
the chocolate milk alone.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:38.280
Mixed in the stomach
with banana, and you get this:

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.680
37 percent less
in your bloodstream,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.480
which shows that
the banana enzyme can still do

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.920
some polyphenol damage
in the stomach.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.440
And, yes, in simulated
stomach acid conditions,

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.240
the polyphenol oxidase enzyme
is indeed down but not out.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.720
So yeah, if you drink
a smoothie with banana in it,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.640
it's better to not let it
sit around.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.760
But even if you drink it
immediately,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.520
you may still get
less polyphenols in your system

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.560
than if you would have
skipped the banana.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.800
But banana makes smoothies
so rich and creamy.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.880
Anything we can add
to the smoothie

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:13.040
to counter the banana
enzyme's effects?

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.160
That's exactly
what I'll cover next.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.920
There is one food that has
more polyphenol oxidase activity

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.120
than a banana,

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.280
and it's a vegetable
that's not a potato.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.640
What vegetable turns brown
that's not a white potato?

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.120
A white mushroom.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.960
White button mushrooms
are packed with that

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.080
polyphenol munching enzyme—
even more so than bananas.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.320
Now, for those of you thinking,
don't worry,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.040
I don't expect to be putting
mushrooms in my smoothies

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.320
anytime soon,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.840
some people add avocado
to smoothies

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.640
or make
a chocolate avocado pudding.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.880
Here's a question:
What if you eat mushrooms

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.000
with a meal packed
with polyphenol-rich foods?

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.480
Might they mix in your stomach
and decrease the absorption

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.920
of the anthocyanins
in red cabbage

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.000
or the berries in dessert,
for instance?

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:02.920
You could also imagine

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:06.000
having some potatoes or eggplant
in a meal.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.880
But note,
this is for fresh produce.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.120
The enzyme is destroyed
by cooking.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.280
And remember,
we should be cooking

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.920
our white mushrooms anyway
because of the agaritine, right?

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.240
So, problem solved.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.360
Who wants to cook their bananas,
though?

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.240
What else can we put
in a smoothie?

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.000
My favorite is mango,
which you can buy frozen

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.560
when it's not in season.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:29.600
Do we have to worry
about it having

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.560
polyphenol-eating enzymes?

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.880
Well, what happens
when you cut open a mango?

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.240
Does it go brown?

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.360
No, so it's probably fine.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.000
And indeed,
when researchers measured

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.800
polyphenol oxidase activity

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.680
in a bunch
of fruits and vegetables,

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:47.400
mango had 500 times less
than banana.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.760
The apple doesn't surprise me,
since it goes brown so fast,

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.840
but what's with the beet greens?

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.520
Better to use
something like kale

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.280
for your green smoothies
instead,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.033
where enzyme activity
is undetectable.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.160
Should you not add bananas
to your oatmeal?

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.200
I no longer add it

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.400
to my cran-chocolate pomegranate
breakfast bowl,

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.280
since I don't want to lose
any of the polyphenols.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.920
But what if you're not making
something chocolatey?

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:15.360
The study only tested
the effects of the enzyme

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.240
on cocoa flavanols.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.800
They didn't check to see
if mixing bananas with berries

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.800
would affect
the berry phytonutrients,

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.880
but the presumption is
that it would.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:28.320
And what does this mean
for bottled smoothie drinks

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.160
that you buy off the shelf
in the store?

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.040
Well, if they've been
pasteurized,

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:34.040
then it should be okay,

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.520
since the heat should destroy
the enzyme.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.360
That's why vegetables are
blanched before they're frozen—

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.240
to destroy the enzymes.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.680
Even just heating banana
to 70°C (160°F),

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:46.640
which is like
the temperature of hot tea,

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.480
for two minutes in the presence
of vitamin C and citric acid

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.680
(which might come
from lemon juice)

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.440
would suppress the enzyme
by 80 percent.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.480
I'm not suggesting drinking
hot smoothies, but rather that,

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:01.080
depending on the ingredients,

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.480
pasteurized smoothie drinks
may not share the same problem

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.800
as fresh-made smoothies.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.120
What about just the vitamin C
and citric acid alone,

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.600
like putting lemon juice
in your smoothie?

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.560
Only cuts the enzyme activity
down by 13 percent.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.200
Works wonders
on a cut apple, though,

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.920
slashing enzyme activity
nearly in half,

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.240
which explains why lemon juice

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.760
can keep your fruit salad
from turning brown.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.440
Anything we can add
to a banana smoothie

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:29.440
to inhibit the enzyme?

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.320
Sulfites used to be put
on fresh fruits and vegetables

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.280
until they were banned in 1986,

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.120
following cases
of sulfite-induced asthma.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.600
They're still used
in dried fruit, though,

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.560
to prevent browning.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:45.600
What about natural agents?

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.840
Onion extracts can prevent
the browning of pears.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:51.920
Both fresh and cooked
onion juice,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.560
but there's got to be a better
option for your smoothie.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.000
Pineapple seems to help keep
apples from browning,

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.040
and bananas too,

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.880
but that was after
soaking in pineapple juice

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:03.640
for three days,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:06.440
so it's not clear
if it would work right away.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.400
There was a study comparing
lemon juice and white wine

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.640
to prevent browning
in pastry dough.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.960
You've heard of hard cider,
hard seltzer?

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.320
The lemon juice beat out
the wine, though,

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:22.520
so better a lemon squeeze
than drinking a hard smoothie.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.520
